      <title>GGZ Gaming Zone: Multiplayer gaming framework</title>
      <subtitle></subtitle>
      <track>Lightning Talks</track>
      <type></type>
      <language></language>
      <abstract>&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Contents of the talk: «Multiplayer gaming through GGZ»&#xA;While many games make use of 3D engines, sound libraries or physics &#xA;simulators, programmers are often wasting time by writing all the networking &#xA;aspects of games by themselves. The GGZ Gaming Zone, which was founded in the &#xA;year 2000 initially to offer a few games for online play, has since turned &#xA;into a provider of powerful libraries which relieve the programmers of those &#xA;tasks. Integrating GGZ into games is easier than ever thanks to the new &#xA;toolkit-aware integration libraries. The talk will highlight some examples &#xA;and also of the challenges of expanding free gaming on the internet.&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA; </abstract>
      <description>&#xA;&lt;p&gt;GGZ Gaming Zone unifies multiplayer capabilities of free games at the &#xA;technical level, while diversifying it on the level of experience by offering &#xA;many cool add-ons to the core game server infrastructure. GGZ powers the KDE &#xA;and GNOME multiplayer frameworks, and is used by Freeciv and other games as &#xA;well.&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA; </description>
      <persons>
          <person id="521">Josef Spillner</person>
